hough the likely winner is unlikely to change.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Ben Ray Luján (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Senator Ben Ray Luján seeks a second term in a state that leans Democratic by about four points. He secured his party's nomination easily and enters the general election with a vast financial advantage, having raised $5.6 million to Larry Marker's roughly $34,000, while outside groups have spent only in his support and nothing against him.\n\nEvery major race rater places this contest in the Safe or Solid Democratic column, and the one published poll, conducted in late August, showed Luján leading 53 to 38 among likely voters. Prediction markets put his chances between 97 and 99 percent. Marker has drawn little funding, outside help, or attention suggesting a competitive race. The open question is the size of the winning margin rather than who wins, with turnout and the national climate the main variables.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9923, "margin": 15.81}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9906, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9896, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9897, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9862, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9853, "margin": 16.0}]}